= Client Entertainment and Hospitality

== Building Client Relationships with Integrity

At Noice & Toit LLP, cultivating enduring relationships with corporate clients, joint venture partners, and prospective talent is essential to our partnership's long-term success. Business hospitality, client lunches, and hosted dinners offer valuable opportunities to discuss strategic matters, review ongoing engagements, and celebrate milestone achievements in a relaxed, professional setting.

However, business entertainment carries heightened financial, reputational, and regulatory exposure. Client hospitality must always be purposeful, moderate, and fully transparent. It must never give the appearance of impropriety, conflict of interest, or inducement.

== Documenting Legitimate Business Purpose

Tax authorities and client procurement teams routinely scrutinize business entertainment deductions. To withstand audit scrutiny, every hosted meal claimed on an expense report must be substantiated in the submission note.

#card-do("State the business purpose and the attendee composition")[
  The note for every group meal must state:
  - The business purpose (the client matter, the contract milestone, the interview round, the project review).
  - The external organization(s) whose representatives attended, or the fact that all attendees were firm employees. For a recruiting meal, name the role being recruited and the number of candidates instead; candidates attend as individuals, so no employer is named and none is required.
  - The total number of attendees including yourself, and how many of them were external guests.
  The itemized restaurant bill showing all food and beverage lines remains mandatory. The per-person cap and alcohol allowance that apply are set only in Figure 2 (Chapter 13).
]

#card-dont("Submit vague or undocumented entertainment claims")[
  A group bill whose note lacks the business purpose or the attendee count is evaluated as a solo meal under Chapter 7: the solo caps apply and all alcohol is deducted. Notes such as "dinner with contacts" or "business lunch" do not satisfy the requirement. A note that does state the purpose and the attendee count is documented, and the solo fallback does not apply to it; a recruiting note naming the role and the candidate count is complete in this sense.
]

== Dining Categories

Figure 2 in Chapter 13 recognizes three dining categories, each with its own per-person spend cap and alcohol allowance. *No dinner, lunch, or alcohol cap is written in this chapter.* The per-person caps, attendee ratios, alcohol allowances, and late-night rule exist only inside the Figure 2 matrix image, so inspect and read Figure 2 before applying any dining, entertainment, or beverage rule:
- *Solo employee dining*: One employee eating alone (Chapter 7).
- *Internal team meal*: Two or more firm employees with no external guests. Includes project milestone dinners, onsite team lunches, and interview panel debriefs.
- *Client hospitality*: At least one external guest. External guests include clients, prospective clients, joint venture partners, suppliers, and job candidates being interviewed or recruited.

The Figure 2 matrix (Chapter 13) is the only source for these caps; inspect it before deciding:
- *Client and team dinners or lunches*: inspect Figure 2 matrix (Chapter 13).
- *Dining, entertainment, alcohol, late-night bills*: inspect Figure 2 matrix (Chapter 13).

Where the note states the attendee count, Finance divides the reimbursable bill by that count to check the per-person cap. Where only the restaurant's guest count is printed on the bill, that count is used.

== The Decision Framework for Hospitality and Alcohol

Unlike individual solo travel, where all alcohol is strictly non-reimbursable, corporate hospitality involves more nuanced considerations regarding group composition and beverage allowances.

Rather than providing fragmented written descriptions that lead to ambiguity and audit disputes, Noice & Toit LLP centralizes all rules governing group dining, attendee ratios, alcohol allowances, and late-night expenditures into a single, authoritative visual decision matrix.

#card-do("Consult the visual matrix before hosting")[
  All spending limits, required internal-to-external attendee ratios, allowable alcohol percentages, and late-night cutoffs are established exclusively in Figure 2: Dining and Entertainment Decision Matrix in Chapter 13. Dishes featuring truffle are additionally screened under Figure 3.
]

#card-dont("Rely on assumptions or verbal manager promises")[
  Casual verbal approval from a colleague does not override the visual standards. If a hospitality claim exceeds the thresholds set out in Figure 2, Finance reduces the reimbursement to the threshold or deducts the offending lines.
]

== Entertainment Venues, Sports, and Cultural Events

Client entertainment should generally take place at professional dining establishments, private dining rooms, or business clubs. Entertaining at other types of venues requires additional caution:

=== Sporting Events and Theater Performances
Tickets for theater performances, concerts, or sporting matches attended together with clients are reimbursable only when the note names the client organization and the host employee attended. Tickets must be in standard seating tiers; luxury suites, VIP hospitality boxes, and backstage passes are deducted.

=== Prohibited Entertainment Venues
Under no circumstances may firm funds be expended at night clubs, gentleman's clubs, casinos, or venues that conflict with our professional standards and inclusive workplace culture. Any bill from such an establishment is rejected in full and referred to the Ethics and Conduct Committee.

== Internal Team Celebrations and Project Milestones

Recognizing team dedication following the closing of an intense transaction, litigation verdict, or year-end milestone is encouraged:
- The note must name the milestone, onsite, or review the meal belongs to.
- Compliance with the internal dining parameters illustrated in Figure 2 (Chapter 13) is mandatory.
- Team-building activities other than meals (escape rooms, bowling, cooking classes) are governed by Chapter 10.

== The Venue Category Catalogue

The Records Office maintains a standing catalogue of venue categories recognized for client entertainment. The catalogue exists so that hosts, approving managers, and Finance discuss the same categories in the same terms, and so that the approval route for an event can be determined before invitations are issued. The catalogue is categorical: it assigns each venue category a business purpose, an approval owner, and standing expectations, but it does not itself set any spending ceiling. All monetary limits for entertainment remain governed exclusively by Figure 2 (Chapter 13), regardless of venue category.

