== Anti-Bribery Case Narratives

The Audit Committee publishes the following narratives from its case files so that hosts can recognize the shape of a problem before it becomes a finding. Details are generalized, and no narrative describes a current engagement. Each narrative carries the same lesson: hospitality becomes an inducement the moment its business purpose grows vague, and vagueness is usually a choice.

In the first narrative, a host invited officials from a permitting authority to a private box at a stadium during a live tender. The invitation was framed as relationship maintenance, but the host could not point to an active matter on which the officials advised, and the timing spoke for itself. The Ethics and Conduct Committee found the hospitality to be an attempt to influence a procurement decision, the claim was rejected in full, and the host was removed from the client relationship. The committee noted that the approval owner named in the venue catalogue had never been asked, which was itself the finding.

In the second narrative, a practice routed a series of dinners for a supplier's sales leadership through a series of modest claims, each individually within the Figure 2 matrix and none individually remarkable. The aggregate pattern, visible only once the register and the claims ledger were read together, showed the supplier had won a renewal in the interim. The Audit Committee treated the sequence as a single orchestrated benefit and referred it to the Ethics and Conduct Committee. Hosts should assume that patterns across claims are visible to the committee, because they are.

In the third narrative, a host accepted, and recorded in the register, season tickets from a client's procurement director. The entry was timely and the attestation was clean, but the committee found that continued acceptance created an expectation of favorable treatment in vendor selection. The tickets were returned with a letter from the general counsel, and the client relationship survived, which the committee attributes to the early and voluntary recording. The lesson the committee draws is its favorite: the register does not create the problem, but it is the only reliable way out of one.

== Event Planning Workflow and Post-Event Review

Client events with more than a handful of external guests follow a standing workflow, regardless of venue category. The workflow exists because most entertainment failures are planning failures: the approval owner was told after the invitations went out, the guest list was assembled from a stale list, or nobody was designated to host on the night. A structured sequence prevents all three.

